    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d285">

                <pb facs="0383" n="373" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg373"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.002/7–2148: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-07-21T16:00:00+09:00">July 21, 1948—4 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 21—10:34 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1334. Air Nanking is full of rumors as to ways and means of persuading
                    Generalissimo to change his way or to make his regime more effective (Shanghai’s
                    1627, July 20, repeated Nanking 1316<anchor n="12" xml:id="d285-f1"/>). No one
                    in Embassy has urged Generalissimo to retire in favor Regency as suggested
                    Shanghai’s telegram.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept 1334, repeated Shanghai 644.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d285-f1" n="12" xml:id="d285-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; reference was made therein to “rumor that US Government has urged
                    Generalissimo to retire in favor of 7-man regency board.” (893.002/7–2048)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
